{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Eb8ReKXLkwkfb8Dv2LTdTy","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/abstract.tex has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87b09203-d53c-40fc-9f4c-444bfc5b012a","uuid":"69e58dba-25ae-423b-877f-015dfaff03b6","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:22:20.182Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/abstract.tex","oldString":"For language modeling, every alternative order is significantly worse than standard frequency BPE (all Holm-adjusted $p \\le 0.032$): bits-per-byte rises by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by up to 9.8\\% on biomedical text.","newString":"For language modeling, merge order clearly matters (all 10 comparisons with Holm-adjusted $p \\le 0.032$): the four non-frequency orders raise bits-per-byte by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text.","originalFile":"Byte-pair encoding (BPE) tokenizers are trained once and then frozen into every model built on them, yet the order of their merges is almost never varied.\nWe ask whether merge order matters, both the order in which merges are \\emph{learned} and the priority with which they are \\emph{applied}.\nWe train six byte-level BPE tokenizers with the same 8,192-entry vocabulary size on the same corpus; they differ only in merge order.\nTwo of them keep the exact vocabulary of standard frequency BPE and change only merge priority.\nFor each tokenizer we pre-train identical 23M-parameter language models on the same 200\\,MB of general and biomedical text, and then fine-tune them on three classification tasks.\nThe answer depends on what we measure.\nFor language modeling, every alternative order is significantly worse than standard frequency BPE (all Holm-adjusted $p \\le 0.032$): bits-per-byte rises by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by up to 9.8\\% on biomedical text.\nEven reordering the \\emph{same} merges costs 1.8--5.7\\%, because short merges fire first and block long ones, leaving 59--79\\% of the vocabulary unused.\nCompression explains this effect: bytes-per-token perfectly rank-predicts general-text bits-per-byte across tokenizers (Spearman $\\rho=-1.0$).\nFor fine-tuned classification, merge order shows no detectable effect: all tokenizers are within $-0.8\\%$ to $+1.6\\%$ of standard BPE, and none of the 15 comparisons is significant.\nA domain-weighted merge order gives a small domain gain (0.8\\% bits-per-byte) and no classification gain.\nMerge order is therefore not a cosmetic detail for generative modeling, but small fine-tuned classifiers are robust to it.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":4,"oldLines":7,"newStart":4,"newLines":7,"lines":[" Two of them keep the exact vocabulary of standard frequency BPE and change only merge priority."," For each tokenizer we pre-train identical 23M-parameter language models on the same 200\\,MB of general and biomedical text, and then fine-tune them on three classification tasks."," The answer depends on what we measure.","-For language modeling, every alternative order is significantly worse than standard frequency BPE (all Holm-adjusted $p \\le 0.032$): bits-per-byte rises by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by up to 9.8\\% on biomedical text.","+For language modeling, merge order clearly matters (all 10 comparisons with Holm-adjusted $p \\le 0.032$): the four non-frequency orders raise bits-per-byte by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text."," Even reordering the \\emph{same} merges costs 1.8--5.7\\%, because short merges fire first and block long ones, leaving 59--79\\% of the vocabulary unused."," Compression explains this effect: bytes-per-token perfectly rank-predicts general-text bits-per-byte across tokenizers (Spearman $\\rho=-1.0$)."," For fine-tuned classification, merge order shows no detectable effect: all tokenizers are within $-0.8\\%$ to $+1.6\\%$ of standard BPE, and none of the 15 comparisons is significant."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Eb8ReKXLkwkfb8Dv2LTdTy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTCxwpnsJ5RigL6id1jR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HASoRjc5vFTTNvEj3xRYB2","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/introduction.tex","old_string":"For language modeling, \\freq beats every alternative.\n\\bpb rises by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text, and all 10 comparisons are significant after Holm correction.","new_string":"For language modeling, merge order clearly matters.\nOn general text, \\freq beats every alternative.\nThe four non-frequency orders raise \\bpb by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text, and all 10 \\bpb comparisons against \\freq are significant after Holm correction."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4173,"cache_read_input_tokens":80716,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4173},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87b09203-d53c-40fc-9f4c-444bfc5b012a","uuid":"fe1b40ab-42d1-412b-9f2b-d862a1174fa4","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:22:22.328Z","request_id":"req_011CgTCxwYvdLkhBgVXVc8wS","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01HASoRjc5vFTTNvEj3xRYB2":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/introduction.tex","old_string":"For language modeling, \\freq beats every alternative.\n\\bpb rises by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text, and all 10 comparisons are significant after Holm correction.","new_string":"For language modeling, merge order clearly matters.\nOn general text, \\freq beats every alternative.\nThe four non-frequency orders raise \\bpb by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text, and all 10 \\bpb comparisons against \\freq are significant after Holm correction."}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HASoRjc5vFTTNvEj3xRYB2","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/introduction.tex has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87b09203-d53c-40fc-9f4c-444bfc5b012a","uuid":"d10a19a6-678c-4a64-b47d-4ebe11696bb6","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:22:22.376Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/introduction.tex","oldString":"For language modeling, \\freq beats every alternative.\n\\bpb rises by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text, and all 10 comparisons are significant after Holm correction.","newString":"For language modeling, merge order clearly matters.\nOn general text, \\freq beats every alternative.\nThe four non-frequency orders raise \\bpb by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text, and all 10 \\bpb comparisons against \\freq are significant after Holm correction.","originalFile":"\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nA BPE tokenizer is a list of merges~\\cite{gage1994new,sennrich2016neural}.\nTraining adds merges to this list one at a time, each time choosing the most frequent adjacent pair.\nAt encoding time, the list order becomes a priority: the tokenizer repeatedly applies the highest-priority merge that fits~\\cite{radford2019language}.\nThe tokenizer is then frozen into every model built on top of it.\nBecause the frequency rule is so standard, merge order is almost never treated as a design choice.\nOur main question is simple: {\\bf does the order of BPE merges change the quality of a model built on the tokenizer?}\n\nThe question matters in practice.\nPractitioners change merge order whenever they re-weight a tokenizer corpus toward a domain, add domain-specific tokens, or merge vocabularies from different sources.\nIf quality is sensitive to merge order, these changes need care.\nIf models are robust to it, merge order can be changed freely, for example for domain adaptation.\nPrior work compares tokenizer \\emph{algorithms}~\\cite{bostrom2020byte,ali2024tokenizer}, vocabulary sizes and compression~\\cite{galle2019investigating,goldman2024unpacking}, intrinsic efficiency measures~\\cite{zouhar2023tokenization}, stochastic segmentation~\\cite{provilkov2020bpe,kudo2018subword}, and domain vocabularies~\\cite{beltagy2019scibert,gu2021domain}.\nHowever, these comparisons change many things at once.\nWe are not aware of a controlled study that varies \\emph{only} merge order while holding data, vocabulary size, model, and training text fixed.\nPrior work also does not separate \\emph{which} merges are learned from the \\emph{priority} with which they are applied.\n\nWe address this gap with a controlled from-scratch study (\\figref{fig:overview}).\nWe train six byte-level BPE tokenizers with 8,192 vocabulary entries on the same corpus, and we vary merge order in two ways.\nFour tokenizers change the \\emph{learning order}, and hence the vocabulary:\nstandard frequency BPE (\\freq), domain-weighted frequency (\\domain), random choice among frequent pairs (\\randlearn), and least-frequent-first (\\revlearn).\nTwo tokenizers change only the \\emph{application order}: they keep \\freq's exact merges and vocabulary, but apply them in a random (\\randorder) or reversed (\\revorder) priority.\nFor each tokenizer, we pre-train identical 23M-parameter GPT-style models on the same 200\\,MB of Wikipedia and PubMed text.\nWe measure tokenizer-independent bits-per-byte (\\bpb), and then fine-tune each model on \\sst, \\agnews, and \\pubmedrct sentence classification.\n\nThe results split cleanly by metric.\nFor language modeling, \\freq beats every alternative.\n\\bpb rises by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text, and all 10 comparisons are significant after Holm correction.\nThe same-vocabulary controls are the most telling.\n\\randorder and \\revorder lose 1.8--5.7\\% \\bpb with \\emph{exactly} the same merges as \\freq, because short merges that fire early consume their inputs and block long, frequent tokens.\nFor example, \\revorder splits ``\\,hypertension'' into eight pieces, while \\freq keeps it as one token.\nCompression explains the whole ranking: bytes-per-token rank-predicts general-text \\bpb perfectly (Spearman $\\rho=-1.0$, $n=6$).\nFor fine-tuned classification, in contrast, every tokenizer lands within $-0.8\\%$ to $+1.6\\%$ of \\freq, and none of the 15 comparisons is significant.\nEven a nearly character-level tokenizer (\\revlearn) is within 1\\% of \\freq on every task.\nDomain weighting buys a real but small domain gain: 0.8\\% \\bpb on PubMed, at a cost of 0.4\\% on general text.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\resizebox{0.95\\linewidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tikzpicture}[\n        box/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, align=center, font=\\small, minimum height=0.9cm, inner sep=4pt},\n        tok/.style={box, fill=blue!6, text width=2.25cm},\n        fam/.style={box, fill=gray!12, font=\\small\\bfseries, text width=3.0cm},\n        out/.style={box, fill=green!8, text width=3.2cm},\n        arr/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}\n    ]\n    \\node[box, fill=yellow!15, text width=2.6cm] (corpus) {Tokenizer corpus\\\\{\\footnotesize 50\\,MB Wiki + PubMed}};\n    \\node[fam, right=0.7cm of corpus, yshift=1.1cm] (learn) {Learning order\\\\{\\footnotesize\\normalfont which merges are learned}};\n    \\node[fam, right=0.7cm of corpus, yshift=-1.1cm] (apply) {Application order\\\\{\\footnotesize\\normalfont same merges as \\freq, new priority}};\n    \\node[tok, right=0.6cm of learn, yshift=0.45cm] (t1) {\\freq, \\domain};\n    \\node[tok, right=0.6cm of learn, yshift=-0.55cm] (t2) {\\randlearn, \\revlearn};\n    \\node[tok, right=0.6cm of apply] (t3) {\\randorder, \\revorder};\n    \\node[box, fill=orange!10, text width=2.7cm, right=3.6cm of corpus] (lm) {Identical 23M LM\\\\{\\footnotesize same 200\\,MB text, 2 seeds}};\n    \\node[out, right=0.7cm of lm, yshift=0.75cm] (bpb) {Held-out bits-per-byte\\\\{\\footnotesize general / biomedical}};\n    \\node[out, right=0.7cm of lm, yshift=-0.75cm] (ft) {Fine-tuned accuracy\\\\{\\footnotesize \\sst, \\agnews, \\pubmedrct}};\n    \\draw[arr] (corpus.east) -- ++(0.3,0) |- (learn.west);\n    \\draw[arr] (corpus.east) -- ++(0.3,0) |- (apply.west);\n    \\draw[arr] (learn.east) -- (t1.west);\n    \\draw[arr] (learn.east) -- (t2.west);\n    \\draw[arr] (apply.east) -- (t3.west);\n    \\draw[arr] (t1.east) -- (lm.west);\n    \\draw[arr] (t2.east) -- (lm.west);\n    \\draw[arr] (t3.east) -- (lm.west);\n    \\draw[arr] (lm.east) -- (bpb.west);\n    \\draw[arr] (lm.east) -- (ft.west);\n    \\end{tikzpicture}}\n    \\caption{Study design. We train six byte-level BPE tokenizers (8,192 entries each) that differ only in merge order. Four change the \\emph{learning order}, which changes the vocabulary. Two keep \\freq's exact merges and change only the \\emph{application order}, \\ie merge priority at encoding time. Every tokenizer is paired with an identical language model trained on the same text, and we evaluate it with tokenizer-independent bits-per-byte and with fine-tuned classification.}\n    \\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\nIn summary, we make the following contributions:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We design a controlled study that isolates BPE merge order, and we separate learning order (which merges exist) from application order (which merges fire first) through same-vocabulary controls.\n    \\item We show that merge order significantly affects language modeling (+1.8--9.8\\% \\bpb) and that pure reordering of an identical vocabulary costs up to 5.7\\%, leaving 59--79\\% of the vocabulary unused.\n    \\item We identify compression as the mechanism: bytes-per-token rank-predicts \\bpb perfectly on general text ($\\rho=-1.0$) and strongly on biomedical text ($\\rho=-0.94$), while R\\'enyi efficiency does not.\n    \\item We find that fine-tuned classification is robust to merge order: our confidence intervals rule out effects larger than about 1.7\\% on \\agnews and \\pubmedrct, and about 3.5\\% on \\sst.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe rest of the paper is organized as follows.\n\\Secref{sec:related} reviews related work.\n\\Secref{sec:method} describes the tokenizers, models, and evaluation.\n\\Secref{sec:results} presents our results, and \\secref{sec:discussion} discusses their implications and limitations.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":26,"oldLines":8,"newStart":26,"newLines":9,"lines":[" We measure tokenizer-independent bits-per-byte (\\bpb), and then fine-tune each model on \\sst, \\agnews, and \\pubmedrct sentence classification."," "," The results split cleanly by metric.","-For language modeling, \\freq beats every alternative.","-\\bpb rises by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text, and all 10 comparisons are significant after Holm correction.","+For language modeling, merge order clearly matters.","+On general text, \\freq beats every alternative.","+The four non-frequency orders raise \\bpb by 1.8--5.8\\% on general text and by 2.8--9.8\\% on biomedical text, and all 10 \\bpb comparisons against \\freq are significant after Holm correction."," The same-vocabulary controls are the most telling."," \\randorder and \\revorder lose 1.8--5.7\\% \\bpb with \\emph{exactly} the same merges as \\freq, because short merges that fire early consume their inputs and block long, frequent tokens."," For example, \\revorder splits ``\\,hypertension'' into eight pieces, while \\freq keeps it as one token."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HASoRjc5vFTTNvEj3xRYB2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTCyb2AoDbwaXZk4Q4No","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now related work and methodology."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1240,"cache_read_input_tokens":84889,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1240},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87b09203-d53c-40fc-9f4c-444bfc5b012a","uuid":"88372b99-7be9-408f-b756-a37a40451294","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:22:23.129Z","request_id":"req_011CgTCyaZ9EiKQrx44NExEY"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTCyb2AoDbwaXZk4Q4No","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015cmQntgmg9nEp2GoXHAc27","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/related_work.tex","content":"\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\para{Comparing tokenizers.}\nBPE started as a compression algorithm~\\cite{gage1994new} and was adapted to subword segmentation for neural machine translation~\\cite{sennrich2016neural}.\nGPT-2 later made it byte-level~\\cite{radford2019language}.\nSeveral studies compare tokenizer families.\n\\citet{bostrom2020byte} find that unigram language-model tokenization~\\cite{kudo2018subword} matches or beats BPE for pre-training.\n\\citet{ali2024tokenizer} show that tokenizer choice affects the downstream performance and training cost of multilingual LLMs.\n\\citet{schmidt2024tokenization} argue that tokenization is more than compression, because tokenizers with similar compression can give different downstream results.\nThese studies vary the algorithm, the vocabulary, and often the training data together.\nUnlike them, we fix the algorithm, the corpus, and the vocabulary size, and we vary only merge order.\nOur same-vocabulary controls go one step further and fix the vocabulary itself.\n\n\\para{Compression and intrinsic measures.}\n\\citet{galle2019investigating} link the success of BPE to shorter sequences.\n\\citet{goldman2024unpacking} show that compression correlates with downstream quality, especially for generation.\n\\citet{zouhar2023tokenization} propose R\\'enyi efficiency as a better predictor of translation quality than compression.\nWe test both measures on tokenizers that differ only in merge order.\nBytes-per-token rank-predicts \\bpb perfectly in our setting, while R\\'enyi efficiency is a much weaker predictor.\nWe also find that the link between compression and quality holds for language modeling but not for fine-tuned classification.\n\n\\para{Stochastic and alternative segmentation.}\nBPE-dropout~\\cite{provilkov2020bpe} and subword regularization~\\cite{kudo2018subword} randomly perturb segmentation during training to improve robustness.\nThese methods change segmentation at training time while keeping the standard segmentation at test time.\nOur \\randorder and \\revorder tokenizers instead fix a different, deterministic merge priority for both training and testing.\nAt the extreme, our \\revlearn tokenizer becomes almost character-level, which connects to byte-level models such as ByT5~\\cite{xue2022byt5}.\nUnlike ByT5, we keep the model and context length fixed, so a shorter-unit tokenizer sees less text per window.\n\n\\para{Domain-specific vocabularies.}\nDomain models such as SciBERT~\\cite{beltagy2019scibert} and PubMedBERT~\\cite{gu2021domain} build vocabularies from in-domain text, and \\citet{gururangan2020don} show that continued domain pre-training helps.\nThese works change the vocabulary and the pre-training data together.\nOur \\domain tokenizer changes only merge order: it up-weights biomedical counts in the same mixed corpus.\nThis isolates how much of a domain gain comes from the tokenizer alone.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1240,"cache_read_input_tokens":84889,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1240},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87b09203-d53c-40fc-9f4c-444bfc5b012a","uuid":"04ccc8fd-e986-49eb-84f5-3d205ec21b9a","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:22:33.193Z","request_id":"req_011CgTCyaZ9EiKQrx44NExEY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015cmQntgmg9nEp2GoXHAc27":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/related_work.tex","content":"\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\para{Comparing tokenizers.}\nBPE started as a compression algorithm~\\cite{gage1994new} and was adapted to subword segmentation for neural machine translation~\\cite{sennrich2016neural}.\nGPT-2 later made it byte-level~\\cite{radford2019language}.\nSeveral studies compare tokenizer families.\n\\citet{bostrom2020byte} find that unigram language-model tokenization~\\cite{kudo2018subword} matches or beats BPE for pre-training.\n\\citet{ali2024tokenizer} show that tokenizer choice affects the downstream performance and training cost of multilingual LLMs.\n\\citet{schmidt2024tokenization} argue that tokenization is more than compression, because tokenizers with similar compression can give different downstream results.\nThese studies vary the algorithm, the vocabulary, and often the training data together.\nUnlike them, we fix the algorithm, the corpus, and the vocabulary size, and we vary only merge order.\nOur same-vocabulary controls go one step further and fix the vocabulary itself.\n\n\\para{Compression and intrinsic measures.}\n\\citet{galle2019investigating} link the success of BPE to shorter sequences.\n\\citet{goldman2024unpacking} show that compression correlates with downstream quality, especially for generation.\n\\citet{zouhar2023tokenization} propose R\\'enyi efficiency as a better predictor of translation quality than compression.\nWe test both measures on tokenizers that differ only in merge order.\nBytes-per-token rank-predicts \\bpb perfectly in our setting, while R\\'enyi efficiency is a much weaker predictor.\nWe also find that the link between compression and quality holds for language modeling but not for fine-tuned classification.\n\n\\para{Stochastic and alternative segmentation.}\nBPE-dropout~\\cite{provilkov2020bpe} and subword regularization~\\cite{kudo2018subword} randomly perturb segmentation during training to improve robustness.\nThese methods change segmentation at training time while keeping the standard segmentation at test time.\nOur \\randorder and \\revorder tokenizers instead fix a different, deterministic merge priority for both training and testing.\nAt the extreme, our \\revlearn tokenizer becomes almost character-level, which connects to byte-level models such as ByT5~\\cite{xue2022byt5}.\nUnlike ByT5, we keep the model and context length fixed, so a shorter-unit tokenizer sees less text per window.\n\n\\para{Domain-specific vocabularies.}\nDomain models such as SciBERT~\\cite{beltagy2019scibert} and PubMedBERT~\\cite{gu2021domain} build vocabularies from in-domain text, and \\citet{gururangan2020don} show that continued domain pre-training helps.\nThese works change the vocabulary and the pre-training data together.\nOur \\domain tokenizer changes only merge order: it up-weights biomedical counts in the same mixed corpus.\nThis isolates how much of a domain gain comes from the tokenizer alone.\n"}}}
