arate these factors further.\nBecause the poor compressors received more compute, we expect such a design to widen, not close, the \\bpb gaps.\n\n\\para{Tasks.}\nOur three tasks are short-text classification with plentiful labels, which likely makes them insensitive to tokenization.\nGeneration, few-shot prompting, spelling or arithmetic tasks, and low-resource fine-tuning may reveal effects that our tasks hide.\n\n\\para{Seeds and domains.}\nWe use 2 pre-training seeds per tokenizer (1 for the \\textsc{-t1} variants), one tokenizer corpus, and one target domain (biomedical).\n\n\\para{Definitions of order.}\nOur definitions of ``random'' and ``reverse'' are design choices: a count threshold of 50 for the learning rules, and dependency-respecting reorderings for application order.\nOther definitions, such as sampling pairs in proportion to frequency, could give milder degradation.\n\n\\para{Fixed fine-tuning hyper-parameters.}\nWe use the same fine-tuning hyper-parameters for every tokenizer.\nThis is fair, but it could slightly disadvantage tokenizers that produce longer sequences, for example through truncation at 256 tokens.\n\n\\para{Broader impact.}\nThis study uses public datasets and small models, and it trains no system intended for deployment.\nOur findings may help practitioners avoid tokenizer edits that silently reduce compression and waste vocabulary, which in turn wastes compute.\nWe do not foresee direct negative uses.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RKWt2aRkFMXNr1dSKQcraA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTD9jae7yQwyb4QhzYLp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B5S9DBdrVgZpCh147MKtAJ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/conclusion.tex","content":"\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe ask whether the order of BPE merges, both learned and applied, affects the models built on a tokenizer.\nIn a controlled study with six tokenizers, 14 language models, and 84 fine-tuning runs, the answer depends on the metric.\nFor language modeling, merge order matters: the four non-frequency orders raise bits-per-byte by 1.8--9.8\\%, all significantly, and even a pure reordering of an identical vocabulary costs up to 5.7\\%.\nCompression fully explains this: the order that keeps common words whole wins.\nFor fine-tuned classification at our scale, merge order does not measurably matter (at most 1.6\\%, not significant).\nDomain-weighted merge order gives a real but small 0.8\\% domain gain.\n\nThe key takeaway is that a merge list is an ordered object: its order decides which tokens are actually used, and so how well a language model can compress text.\n\nSeveral follow-ups would test how far these findings extend:\nequal-compute and equal-byte-context controls;\nmodels with more than 100M parameters and 32k vocabularies;\ngeneration, few-shot, and low-data (100--1,000 example) evaluations;\nand a rarer target domain, such as chemistry or code, where domain-biased merges may help more.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2551,"cache_read_input_tokens":103477,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2551},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87b09203-d53c-40fc-9f4c-444bfc5b012a","uuid":"fbd4edc7-1786-4eed-8a00-ff55a29acca8","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:25:01.174Z","request_id":"req_011CgTD9izBMv8pPxCB5NvFw","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01B5S9DBdrVgZpCh147MKtAJ":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/conclusion.tex","content":"\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe ask whether the order of BPE merges, both learned and applied, affects the models built on a tokenizer.\nIn a controlled study with six tokenizers, 14 language models, and 84 fine-tuning runs, the answer depends on the metric.\nFor language modeling, merge order matters: the four non-frequency orders raise bits-per-byte by 1.8--9.8\\%, all significantly, and even a pure reordering of an identical vocabulary costs up to 5.7\\%.\nCompression fully explains this: the order that keeps common words whole wins.\nFor fine-tuned classification at our scale, merge order does not measurably matter (at most 1.6\\%, not significant).\nDomain-weighted merge order gives a real but small 0.8\\% domain gain.\n\nThe key takeaway is that a merge list is an ordered object: its order decides which tokens are actually used, and so how well a language model can compress text.\n\nSeveral follow-ups would test how far these findings extend:\nequal-compute and equal-byte-context controls;\nmodels with more than 100M parameters and 32k vocabularies;\ngeneration, few-shot, and low-data (100--1,000 example) evaluations;\nand a rarer target domain, such as chemistry or code, where domain-biased merges may help more.\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B5S9DBdrVgZpCh147MKtAJ","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/conclusion.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87b09203-d53c-40fc-9f4c-444bfc5b012a","uuid":"255aee4d-dc4f-400c-b854-286baf51f591","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:25:01.224Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/sections/conclusion.tex","content":"\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe ask whether the order of BPE merges, both learned and applied, affects the models built on a tokenizer.\nIn a controlled study with six tokenizers, 14 language models, and 84 fine-tuning runs, the answer depends on the metric.\nFor language modeling, merge order matters: the four non-frequency orders raise bits-per-byte by 1.8--9.8\\%, all significantly, and even a pure reordering of an identical vocabulary costs up to 5.7\\%.\nCompression fully explains this: the order that keeps common words whole wins.\nFor fine-tuned classification at our scale, merge order does not measurably matter (at most 1.6\\%, not significant).\nDomain-weighted merge order gives a real but small 0.8\\% domain gain.\n\nThe key takeaway is that a merge list is an ordered object: its order decides which tokens are actually used, and so how well a language model can compress text.\n\nSeveral follow-ups would test how far these findings extend:\nequal-compute and equal-byte-context controls;\nmodels with more than 100M parameters and 32k vocabularies;\ngeneration, few-shot, and low-data (100--1,000 example) evaluations;\nand a rarer target domain, such as chemistry or code, where domain-biased merges may help more.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01B5S9DBdrVgZpCh147MKtAJ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTD9jae7yQwyb4QhzYLp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UbWw1k4fAqfujV5VWSAmHx","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/appendix/appendix.tex","content":"\\section{Additional Results}\n\\label{app:extra}\n\n\\para{Training curves.}\n\\Figref{fig:train_curves} shows the per-byte training loss of the seed-0 models.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.7\\linewidth]{figures/train_curves.png}\n    \\caption{Approximate per-byte training loss (seed 0) against the fraction of the shared training text seen. The curves overlap for most of training; the gaps open late, as \\freq and \\domain keep improving. Labels use underscores (\\eg \\texttt{REV\\_LEARN} is \\revlearn).}\n    \\label{fig:train_curves}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\para{Compute.}\n\\Tabref{tab:compute} lists the training tokens, steps, and wall-clock time of each model.\nBecause all models see the same text, poor compressors take more steps.\nWall-clock times come from shared GPUs and are only a rough guide.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Pre-training compute for seed 0. Compute is relative to \\freq (same model, so proportional to tokens). Poor compressors receive 1.7--3.8$\\times$ more compute yet still reach worse \\bpb.}\n    \\label{tab:compute}\n    \\small\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Tokenizer} & \\textbf{Train tokens} & \\textbf{Steps} & \\textbf{Minutes} & \\textbf{Rel. compute} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        \\freq                  & 51.0M  & 1,555 & 24.3 & 1.00$\\times$ \\\\\n        \\domain                & 52.4M  & 1,600 & 18.4 & 1.03$\\times$ \\\\\n        \\randlearn             & 110.0M & 3,358 & 33.2 & 2.16$\\times$ \\\\\n        \\randlearn\\textsc{-t1} & 112.7M & 3,438 & 56.8 & 2.21$\\times$ \\\\\n        \\revlearn              & 194.6M & 5,939 & 32.4 & 3.82$\\times$ \\\\\n        \\randorder             & 86.4M  & 2,637 & 28.6 & 1.70$\\times$ \\\\\n        \\randorder\\textsc{-t1} & 91.3M  & 2,787 & 45.4 & 1.79$\\times$ \\\\\n        \\revorder              & 116.1M & 3,542 & 37.9 & 2.28$\\times$ \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\para{Macro-F1.}\n\\Tabref{tab:f1} reports macro-F1, which tells the same story as accuracy.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Fine-tuned macro-F1 (\\%, mean $\\pm$ sd over 4 runs) and relative change against \\freq. Best mean per task in \\textbf{bold}. Mean test length in tokens is shown in parentheses, which reflects compression.}\n    \\label{tab:f1}\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccccccc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\sst} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\agnews} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\pubmedrct} \\\\\n        \\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7} \\cmidrule(lr){8-10}\n        \\textbf{Tokenizer} & F1 & $\\Delta$ & (len) & F1 & $\\Delta$ & (len) & F1 & $\\Delta$ & (len) \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        \\freq      & 80.2 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.4} & ---          & (30)  & \\textbf{89.3} {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.4} & ---          & (70)  & \\textbf{75.4} {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.5} & ---          & (37) \\\\\n        \\domain    & \\textbf{81.4} {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.4} & +1.57\\%      & (31)  & 88.9 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.4} & $-$0.48\\%    & (74)  & 75.4 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.2} & $-$0.08\\%    & (34) \\\\\n        \\randlearn & 79.6 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.6} & $-$0.69\\%    & (59)  & 88.8 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.4} & $-$0.58\\%    & (131) & 75.1 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.6} & $-$0.49\\%    & (83) \\\\\n        \\revlearn  & 80.7 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.7} & +0.66\\%      & (104) & 88.7 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.4} & $-$0.72\\%    & (213) & 74.8 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.2} & $-$0.91\\%    & (140) \\\\\n        \\randorder & 79.7 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.7} & $-$0.54\\%    & (47)  & 88.9 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.2} & $-$0.51\\%    & (108) & 74.9 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.8} & $-$0.67\\%    & (67) \\\\\n        \\revorder  & 80.0 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.9} & $-$0.23\\%    & (63)  & 88.9 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.4} & $-$0.47\\%    & (139) & 74.6 {\\scriptsize $\\pm$ 0.4} & $-$1.15\\%    & (87) \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\para{Implementation details.}\nOur BPE trainer keeps a dictionary of pre-token counts and updates pair counts incrementally after each merge.\nFor the application-order tokenizers, we compute a dependency graph in which each merge points to the merges that use its output symbol, and we draw a topological order of this graph: uniformly at random for \\randorder, and preferring the latest-learned available merge for \\revorder.\nAll experiments ran on shared NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPUs, using about 3\\,GB of memory per job; each language model took 18--57 minutes.\nWe used PyTorch, HuggingFace \\texttt{tokenizers}, NumPy, SciPy, pandas, and matplotlib.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2551,"cache_read_input_tokens":103477,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2551},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87b09203-d53c-40fc-9f4c-444bfc5b012a","uuid":"b61a0610-7cc5-4443-b5a5-2ac4c68cac7d","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:25:17.869Z","request_id":"req_011CgTD9izBMv8pPxCB5NvFw","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01UbWw1k4fAqfujV5VWSAmHx":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/tokenizer_merge_order_20261027_120001_ef56gh78/paper_draft/appendix/appendix.tex","content":"\\section{Additional Results}\n\\label{app:extra}\n\n\\para{Training 